    "body": "> > Anne convinced me that #9048 shouldn't be needed anymore.\r\n> \r\n> Thanks. Even when `showPopover()` is called? That's the only time it now seems like it might still help to call \"check and possibly close popover stack\". I'm thinking in this example:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> <div id=p1 popover>\r\n>   <button id=b popovertarget=b></button>\r\n> </div>\r\n> <div id=p2 popover>\r\n>   <div id=p3 popover></div>\r\n> </div>\r\n> <script>\r\n> p1.showPopover();\r\n> p2.showPopover(); // Popover stack is now: p1, p2\r\n> p2.appendChild(b);\r\n> b.popoverTarget = a; // This reverses the ancestor relationships\r\n> p3.showPopover(); // P1 closes here\r\n> </script>\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> At the end, p1 closes because the ancestor relationship is p2->p1 and p2->p3, so opening p3 closes p1. Perhaps that's fine. This isn't a great example, since it seems \"fine\". I'm just wondering if there are worse examples where bad things happen. Maybe not?\r\n\r\n@annevk Can you elaborate your reasoning here?",
